	('b511c6ce-d039-42c1-bd83-daed160886d1', 'f75f7f16-84b9-41ce-b842-2a77a9dd70b8', NULL, 'What you two modeled here? It’s evidence of high-impact educational leadership.

🧠 Cognitive Reframing in Action
“When they had to quantify how hard it was, it made it more manageable.” – Elisa Strong

That''s a textbook example of cognitive reframing, used in leadership and behavioral science to reduce overwhelm and build action. You''re not just leading — you''re applying evidence-based techniques that help teachers move from stuck to forward.

💪 Collective Efficacy in Motion
“You don’t always get feedback... it feels one-way.” – Alexander Vermaire

Yet both of you kept showing up: checking in, setting expectations, being culture thermostats. That’s collective efficacy — belief that together, you can make the hard things doable. Research links that directly to stronger student outcomes.', 'idea', '2025-04-08 21:30:07.33074+00', '2025-04-08 21:30:07.33074+00', NULL, NULL),
	('50aa9f6b-2563-46ac-95b8-0b7de20c9b8d', 'bd47a56c-b275-4972-987f-30b0cd993f17', NULL, '“Wake Up, Folks”: Rethinking Teaching and Bias in the Age of AI
By Insight Fly 2025, grounded in the conversation of Tom Thorpe and Jeff Crawford
In a 30-minute Sideby session that wove together storytelling, challenge, laughter, and serious reflection, two educators—Tom Thorpe and Jeff Crawford—stepped into the heart of a conversation many are just beginning to navigate: What does AI mean for the identity, agency, and craft of teaching?
From the very beginning, their stories signaled this wasn’t going to be a typical tech talk. This was a co-thinking space, where AI was examined not just for what it can do, but for what it reveals about what educators value.

Enhancing Identity and Agency: A Different Kind of AI Story
One of the most compelling moments in the conversation came when Tom recounted helping a student—a senior with a complex home life and a strong internal drive—use ChatGPT to write a business plan. This wasn’t for a grade or assignment. It was deeply personal. Her goal? To impress her dad.
Rather than approach the situation with suspicion or concern about “cheating,” Tom recognized what was really at stake: the student’s identity, her longing to be seen, and her right to shape how she showed up in that relationship. He reframed the task, internally thinking: “Let’s ChatGPT the hell out of this so you can impress your dad.”
What followed wasn’t just a polished plan—it was a catalyst for conversation between the student and her father, including discussions about college. It became a moment of visibility, confidence, and empowerment.
Tom''s approach underscores a critical insight: when educators prioritize connection, context, and care, AI becomes a tool for deepening student identity—not diminishing it. He wasn’t outsourcing the learning. He was leveraging the tool to support a bigger human need: to feel seen, taken seriously, and full of potential.
As he put it, "It helped her have a great conversation with her dad... and he was like, oh, you''re for real, like... you could actually go and do stuff." The technology wasn''t the point. The transformation was.

AI Won’t Replace Teachers—Unless We Do
As the session unfolded, both educators confronted a familiar tension: fear that AI will replace teachers. But instead of brushing it off or rushing to reassure, they examined the roots of that fear. Tom offered this: “If you think you''re going to get replaced by ChatGPT... if that''s the only thing that we do as teachers... then yes, that probably would be replaced.” The fear, in his view, wasn’t really about AI—it was about undervaluing the real complexity and humanity of the profession.
Jeff added nuance, noting how even teachers themselves can inadvertently position their roles around low-level, repetitive tasks: “If our jobs are allocated and our roles and the things that we do are menial, repetitive tasks... then we''re not being creative or we''re not being human.”
Both agreed: if what educators do can be fully programmed, then it probably should be. But that''s not what powerful teaching looks like. "Wake up, folks," Tom said. *"That is not your job, and it''s never been our job."

Seeing Bias: In AI, In Ourselves, In Our Systems
The conversation took a reflective turn as they began examining bias—not just in AI-generated content, but in their own classroom practices.
Jeff recounted a moment when he asked a GPT to adapt a well-designed math lesson. The AI suggested beginning with a summary and pre-teaching approach that directly contradicted learner-centered practices. "The GPT said pre teach and preload, which totally took the position of the learners... and made them passive."
This wasn’t a case of AI hallucination—it was bias embedded in the training data. Common practice had become mistaken for best practice. “Bias isn''t just about race or gender,” Jeff explained. “It''s about bad pedagogy becoming authority because it''s common.”
Tom took the reflection further, imagining AI tools being used not just as generators but as real-time analytic mirrors. He described a moment in a classroom where a discussion had gone awry and wondered aloud: what if AI could visually trace the conversational thread, help unpack where it went sideways, and guide reflection?
They envisioned AI not as a content machine, but as a co-facilitator—mapping who speaks, whose metaphors dominate, and which students remain on the margins because the conversation never reached them. “Let’s look at the thread of the conversation,” Tom said. “Why might Jeff not be talking? Well, because we were like only like giving sports, like basketball metaphors.”

Teaching Isn’t Simple—And That’s the Point
Throughout their exchange, Tom and Jeff returned to a deeper truth: the value of teaching lies in its complexity. Simplifying it to inputs and outputs undermines not just the profession but students’ humanity.
Tom voiced a powerful concern that surfaced again and again: if educators internalize the belief that their primary value lies in performing routine, easily replicable tasks, they may inadvertently reinforce the very narrative that makes them feel replaceable. As he put it, “If we want kids to be simple and we want teachers to be simple, then we’re just perpetuating that fear and locking it in.”
The conversation challenged the very notion of teaching as a checklist of deliverables. Jeff named a clear tension: “If our jobs are allocated and our roles and the things that we do are menial, repetitive tasks... then we''re not being creative or we''re not being human.” Tom echoed this, pushing deeper into the emotional root of that fear: “The fear is that teachers don''t value what they do.” In other words, if educators come to believe that their value lies solely in delivering content or managing procedures, they begin to believe that anything—or anyone—could do it.
This mindset also shapes pedagogy. Jeff described how some teachers may see their work as teaching “repetitive simple steps” to solve equations or follow procedures, which risks turning students into passive recipients. Tom agreed, and with urgency added, “If that''s like what you think you do and that''s the value you bring, and that''s how you look at your work…” then of course it feels replaceable. But that’s never been the real work.
They also turned their critique toward how these dynamics show up in AI tools themselves. Jeff recounted asking a GPT to adapt a strong math lesson, only to find it defaulting to surface-level, teacher-centered strategies. “The GPT said pre teach and preload, which totally took the position of the learners... and made them passive.” Tom recognized the danger immediately—not just that the tool was wrong, but that it was replicating common practices that are already misaligned. “You''re doing that thing where you''re just… preloading,” he said, calling out how often this impulse—whether from a person or a machine—can deny students their right to struggle, question, and grow.
“Bias isn''t just about race or gender,” Jeff reflected. “It''s about bad pedagogy becoming authority because it''s common.” Tom took it further, wondering aloud what it would look like if AI could help us trace those moments in our conversations or lessons—when we pivoted away from student ownership, when we leaned too hard on what’s comfortable or familiar. What if, he asked, we could map those decisions and reflect on them in real time?
"Wake up folks," Tom said. "That is not your job, and it''s never been our job." The real job, as their conversation revealed, is to facilitate thinking, not just deliver answers. It’s to build conditions for voice, curiosity, and confidence—not just compliance. It''s to co-create meaning with students in ways that can’t be scripted. Tom’s words served as both a challenge and an affirmation: the most important parts of teaching—the relational, the responsive, the human—can’t be automated. And they never should be.

✏️ Three Practical Actions Tom or Jeff Might Take Using AI Tools
Craft a Reflective LinkedIn Post from This Conversation
 Use this transcript with an AI assistant to write a short post sharing your insights on AI, bias, and the irreplaceable value of teaching. Title prompt: “What AI Can’t Replace” or “The Fear Behind the Fear.”


Build a Bias Mapping Protocol
 Use an AI tool to help develop a protocol that maps classroom dialogue: Who speaks? What metaphors are used? What biases might be surfacing? Pair it with real-time classroom transcripts or recordings to build teacher and student reflection.


Co-create a GPT With Students
 Instead of using a generic AI model, use an assistant to co-build a custom GPT with your students—trained on your class values, equity commitments, and preferred teaching practices. Call it Bobby, as Jeff joked, and let it reflect your learning community.



If this article sparked ideas or reflections of your own, we invite you to carry the conversation forward—whether through your next AI interaction, classroom dialogue, or faculty meeting. Because, as Tom and Jeff reminded us, our work is not simple, and that’s exactly why it matters.', 'idea', '2025-04-09 17:19:55.346308+00', '2025-04-09 17:19:55.346308+00', NULL, NULL),
