	('23aff998-ca93-42e9-a625-e7c08707c1ed', '43812edc-e7fd-4226-bb9c-cd9bd174e0b0', NULL, 'Mike Peck
Technologist | Systems Thinker | Urgency-Fueled Optimist

“AI is often a starting point in my workflow.”
“We need to become problem seekers, not just problem solvers.”
“We’re not steering a dinghy—we’re steering a cruise ship.”

Mike Peck is the kind of educator who lives at the intersection of strategy and imagination, someone who not only explores the future—but actively prototypes it. With a deep fluency in AI and systems-level thinking, he’s not content to simply integrate new tools into old models. Instead, he asks bigger questions: What becomes possible when AI is not just an add-on, but the starting point for how we plan, analyze, and create?

His reflection reveals a personal shift already underway. “Massively changed the way I approach teaching and learning,” he notes, describing how AI has transformed his workflows around strategy, process, synthesis, and content creation. For Mike, this isn’t theoretical—it’s hands-on. He’s experimenting in real time, bringing a designer’s mindset to both his own work and the systems he leads.

When he says, “We need to become problem seekers, not just problem solvers,” he’s articulating a fundamental shift in what we prepare learners to do in an AI-saturated world. It’s a call to move beyond reactive thinking—to cultivate curiosity, creativity, and the ability to define the right problems before racing to solve them. It echoes the mindset of inquiry-based learning, where discovery is prioritized over delivery.

Mike doesn’t shy away from complexity. In fact, he names one of his biggest struggles as “tempering speed.” While he feels the urgency to move faster, he acknowledges that meaningful change requires steering large, complex systems with care. “You’re not steering a dinghy,” he reflects. “You’re steering a cruise ship.” This awareness shows not just technical fluency but emotional intelligence and systems awareness—hallmarks of a thoughtful leader.

Even in the face of friction, Mike stays grounded in purpose. He balances ambition with caution, speed with stewardship. And through it all, he keeps circling back to the idea that what matters most is how we prepare people—not just systems—for the future. That’s what makes Mike more than just a tech-savvy leader. He’s a human-centered educator working to widen what’s possible.

3 Ideas to Think About
“We need to become problem seekers, not just problem solvers.”
This shift repositions learners from task completers to curious architects of inquiry. What structures and routines support this stance in schools?

“We’re not steering a dinghy—we’re steering a cruise ship.”
A powerful metaphor for change leadership. How do we lead with urgency while also honoring the pace, process, and people that systems change requires?

“AI is often a starting point in my workflow.”
Mike’s reflection offers a model of productive co-agency with AI. How might we support other educators in designing workflows where AI is a launchpad for creativity rather than a shortcut to answers?

Authors and Thinkers to Consider
Sherry Turkle – Reclaiming Conversation: On the human cost of technological acceleration and the need for deeper dialogue.

Zaretta Hammond – Culturally Responsive Teaching and the Brain: On identity, agency, and designing learning experiences that are both rigorous and relational.

Benjamin Bratton – The Stack: On new architectures of governance, computation, and civilization—relevant to Mike’s “next tier of civilization” reflections.', 'idea', '2025-04-15 21:43:58.356695+00', '2025-04-15 21:43:58.356695+00', NULL, NULL),
	('1b465de8-92d5-4dd8-bcbf-29e42baafd68', 'd3be9b68-297b-416b-828a-eabddea74cb7', NULL, 'test idea at 1:34pm', 'idea', '2025-04-14 17:34:59.86887+00', '2025-04-14 17:34:59.86887+00', NULL, NULL),
