	('aff910fa-cb42-45aa-b975-f0e3d6326383', 'cd985910-e971-4907-988f-d311b2adb4d1', NULL, 'Rob Shaw: Building Shared Wisdom, Empowering Each Educator
Current Stance & Voice
 Rob Shaw is a reflective and steady presence in the educator community—a teacher whose sense of purpose has deepened over time. “The further I’m in,” he says, referring to his ongoing journey through the profession, “the more I think I can change it and make it better”​upduo-session-138221-20…. He brings a quiet confidence, grounded in lived experience and sustained by curiosity. Rob speaks not only with care for his students but with a deep desire to support fellow educators—especially by sharing the kinds of insights, tools, and encouragement he once wished he had himself.
Foundations First: Guaranteeing Readiness for What Comes Next
 Rob’s approach to learning centers on a single, powerful belief: every student deserves a solid foundation. He questions systems that move learners along too quickly, describing how time-based expectations often leave gaps that compound over time. “If we’re building a house and we only get 70% of that foundation created,” he explains, “the structure weakens.” Instead, Rob advocates for mastery-based learning—allowing time to vary in service of deep understanding. His vision is clear: ensure that every student is truly grounded in foundational skills and knowledge before asking them to do next-level things.
Rob also emphasizes the importance of differentiated pacing not just as remediation, but as enrichment. When some students demonstrate early mastery, he believes in giving them space to explore—inviting them into new challenges or independent investigations while he provides the extra time and support others need to reach full understanding. In this way, no student is held back or left behind. Everyone learns. Everyone moves forward.
Shared Learning, Shared Power: Becoming Smarter Together
 Rob draws an insightful connection between today’s most advanced self-driving vehicles and the future of educator collaboration. Modern cars no longer rely on isolated sensors—they share experiences through networked AI, learning from billions of miles logged by others. Rob imagines a similar possibility for educators: “If people are willing to get on sideby and learn from each other,” he says, “we could combine all of our ideas into something powerful”​
In this vision, each educator’s practice becomes a data point in a collective intelligence. Lessons learned, strategies tested, missteps included—they’re all valuable. When shared, they form a kind of communal GPS for professional learning. “A shared brain,” Rob calls it. Not to prescribe one way of teaching, but to help each educator—new or veteran—navigate the challenges ahead with greater insight, perspective, and possibility. In this shared model, no one is alone. Everyone contributes. Everyone grows.

Three Pathway Challenges for Growth
Designing for Mastery, Not Timelines
 Rob has the philosophy—now it’s about turning it into practice. How might he design or share scalable, flexible systems that let students proceed only when truly grounded in key understanding?


Creating the Conditions for Collective Educator Intelligence
 Rob sees the future of professional learning as collaborative and networked. What might it look like to help educators share field-tested strategies and lessons in a way that builds shared insight?


Empowering Fellow Educators to See Themselves as Contributors
 Rob’s humility and clarity make him an ideal guide for helping other educators find their voice, share their wisdom, and recognize the power of their lived experience. How might he play a role in lifting up others?', 'idea', '2025-04-22 16:11:03.7262+00', '2025-04-22 16:11:03.7262+00', NULL, NULL),
	('61a8ef7c-f880-4729-b430-7422379b16b8', '1e39285c-a332-4528-9b84-5b399b1863a8', NULL, 'What Matters Now: Presence, Pattern, and Power in the Age of AI
Educator Dox Brown on how AI can help us teach better—not by doing more, but by showing up more fully.

In a conversation that wove together systems thinking, snowboarding metaphors, and some hard truths about the future of learning, Dox Brown and Jeff Crawford stepped into deep terrain. This wasn’t a session about AI tools. It was a wake-up call—an invitation to reconsider the purpose of teaching, the presence of educators, and the possibility of technology as a partner, not a threat.
What emerged was a bold reframing of what learning will need to look like when AI becomes ubiquitous—and what roles only humans can play.


What Matters Now: How We Show Up
Dox Brown isn’t speculating about the future of teaching—he’s describing what’s already changing.
In a world where AI can generate lesson plans, feedback, and written documents in seconds, he believes we’re entering a new phase of education. One where the emphasis shifts from producing artifacts—like written assessments, portfolios, and reports—to how we engage with one another in real time.
“All your written artifacts can be done by AI now… What matters next is how you show up in that space.”
Dox calls this the beginning of a pedagogy of presence—a shift in focus toward what happens in the moment, between people. Not the planning, but the choices. The attention. The way an educator invites thinking, holds space, and responds with care.
This isn’t about becoming more efficient. It’s about becoming more human in the spaces where learning actually unfolds.
For Dox, presence is not just a disposition—it’s a practice. A way of honoring the complexity of learning by being fully there for the people in the room.

The Skills That Matter More, Not Less
Dox doesn’t fear that AI will replace teachers.
 He fears that educators might undervalue the parts of their work that are irreplaceable.
“This might be the first time in history that the skills of an educator have high market value.”
But not all skills. The ones that matter now are the ones that can’t be templated:
“All your written artifacts can be done by AI now... What matters next is how you show up in that space.”
That space is relational. It’s live. It’s unfolding in the moment.
 Teaching, as Dox sees it, is increasingly about presence—about the ability to connect, to facilitate meaning-making, and to adapt with integrity and care.
It’s not about controlling outcomes. It’s about being fully human alongside others who are still becoming.

From Optimization to Understanding
As the conversation turned toward community and connection, Dox offered a striking insight:
“How do we keep coming back to each other? To be understood rather than to be heard.”
For Dox, the problem isn’t whether voices are present in the system. It’s whether we’re still creating the conditions to make meaning together.
He’s not focused on making AI more personalized—he’s focused on making it more relational. He envisions tools that reflect how our ideas move, how our thinking patterns unfold, and where our conversations fracture or connect.
In Dox’s frame, understanding is not something to automate.
 It’s something we cultivate, slowly, by returning to one another with curiosity and care.

AI as a Mirror, Not a Replacement
One of Dox’s most revealing comments came with a mix of humility and humor:
“AI is the only thing I know of in the universe that doesn’t get tired of me.”
He’s not using AI to bypass the work. He’s using it to support it.
 Dox has designed GPT-powered tools to help early-career educators plan a year of lessons aligned to their values. He’s built assistants to speed up repetitive tasks. He’s even shaped AI into a kind of thinking partner—something that can reflect his patterns back to him over time.
What he values most isn’t what the tool produces—it’s what the process reveals.
Dox imagines AI not as a content engine, but as an epistemic cartographer—a mapper of thought, a companion in reflection. Something that helps us see how we think, how we connect, and how we grow.

A Closing Shift in Focus
Dox Brown isn’t worried that AI will overpower education.
 He’s more concerned it will isolate us—unless we design it to bring us back to one another.
“How do we keep coming back to each other? To be understood rather than to be heard.”
For Dox, the promise of AI isn’t in personalization or automation. It’s in amplifying understanding—helping us map how we think, where we diverge, and how meaning is made across differences.
He imagines tools that don’t just generate content, but reflect back our patterns of discourse. Tools that can say: Here’s how your thinking connects to others. Here’s where you might agree. Here’s where you’re not seeing each other yet.
That’s not about replacing humans.
 It’s about designing technology to serve our deepest human need: to make meaning together.', 'idea', '2025-04-22 22:42:27.41548+00', '2025-04-22 22:42:27.41548+00', NULL, NULL),
	('695ec87f-572f-46d6-8720-2961669587ca', '59e9f386-0ecc-4201-99dd-802f7f8e7b33', NULL, 'Math Coach | Advocate for Growth Mindset & Equitable Access

“My why is to decrease barriers to access.”

Nova Katz brings a deep sense of purpose to her role as a math coach, specializing in elementary mathematics. Her work centers on supporting teachers in using multiple representations—manipulatives, visual models, and connections between strategies—to deepen student understanding. Her belief in the capability of all learners and educators shines through her stance: just as every student can learn rigorous math, every teacher can teach it. She sees her role as a partner and resource-gatherer—someone who does the deep reading and research to make it actionable for busy teachers.

Three Ideas to Think About

“Everyone can.” Nova holds a dual belief in the potential of every student and every teacher to meet high expectations.

Teaching is relational and empowering. She positions herself as someone who helps teachers see their own strengths and trust their professional intuition.

Flow fuels her. Whether through spinning ceramics, strategic board games, or knitting, Nova values activities that immerse her in creative and contemplative states—mirroring the kind of deep engagement she cultivates in classrooms.', 'idea', '2025-04-22 22:44:48.770687+00', '2025-04-22 22:44:48.770687+00', NULL, NULL),
	('9c171c38-c0f9-4ec3-a311-cd0e280f986b', 'e703c834-b010-4c88-9b89-e310df8ad353', NULL, '🧠 Jason Fernandez
Professor | Social Worker | AI Educator

Jason is a professor of psychology at a community college and teaches graduate-level social work. With over 13 years in higher education, he brings both academic insight and grounded practitioner experience to the conversation. Recently, he’s stepped into a pioneering role as an AI technology consultant at the Graduate College of Social Work, where he’s co-launched a platform called 60 Watts AI Faculty Lounge. The initiative aims to serve as a “companion” for educators, helping them thoughtfully integrate AI into their curriculum at their own pace.

Three Ideas to Think With
Learner + Teacher = Co-Learners:
“The learner and teacher are like this now. We are both in the same space… we are both struggling trying to figure out how to integrate AI into our lives.” Jason challenges traditional hierarchies in education, suggesting that teachers and students are now co-navigators in the age of AI.

AI for Integrity, Not Just Output:
He envisions AI tools that do more than automate tasks—ones that maintain “integrity” and are responsive to learners’ individual pacing, particularly in K–12. He’s driven to build systems that are ethical, not extractive.

Obsessed and All-In:
“I have been obsessed with AI since… last October or August and that’s all I ever do now.” Jason’s passion is palpable. Whether exploring AI or playing the trumpet, his commitment to curiosity and craft shapes how he shows up across roles.', 'idea', '2025-04-24 18:05:20.197307+00', '2025-04-24 18:05:20.197307+00', NULL, NULL),
	('bfc6530d-f1b5-4270-a0dc-3cb90c647a2f', '59e9f386-0ecc-4201-99dd-802f7f8e7b33', NULL, 'Nova Katz: Searching for Meaning, Shaping What’s Next
Quarter-Page Insight | April 2025

Nova is at a crossroads—brimming with possibility, but hungry for purpose. Though her current coaching role offers stability (and tuition for her kids), she feels underutilized and disconnected from impact: “My work expects nothing of me... but I want to actually make the world a better place.” In response, she’s pushing outward—investing energy in the BTC conference, diving into international consulting work, and exploring a potential role on Peter’s emerging European team.

Her passion is crystallizing: she wants to support other instructional coaches, especially those in elementary math. “That''s where I feel like I''m at my best,” she shared, dreaming aloud about becoming a “coach’s coach.” She’s also deepening her facilitation chops through parent education series, leveraging GPT-4 as a design partner for interactive sessions. And in a moment that perfectly blends the practical and the playful, she’s using AI to generate optimized student groupings for her school’s 4th grade survival-style camping trip—testing the tool’s limits and learning how to frame structure with clarity.

Despite lingering questions (“Am I pigeonholing myself?”), Nova is actively shaping her path forward—with courage, care, and strategic imagination.

Three Pathway Challenges Nova Might Explore
Designing the Niche: How might Nova formalize her vision of becoming a coach’s coach, and begin building offerings or collaborations that make it real?

Balancing Mission + Stability: How might she reconcile her financial and family priorities with her drive to grow, experiment, and lead?

Positioning AI as a Co-Thinker: How might Nova continue to refine her use of AI—not just for tasks, but as a reflective thought partner across design, facilitation, and strategy?', 'idea', '2025-04-24 18:14:12.521412+00', '2025-04-24 18:14:12.521412+00', NULL, NULL),
	('cc3b7381-5107-4c71-bc19-edeaaa3ad861', '59e9f386-0ecc-4201-99dd-802f7f8e7b33', NULL, 'Nova Katz on Coaching, Campfires, and ChatGPT: Learning Out Loud with AI
Nova Katz isn’t waiting for professional development to happen to her—she’s making her own. Fueled by a drive to grow and contribute meaningfully, she’s been leveraging AI as a trusted partner in the work that matters most to her.
“I’m a team of one. But I’ve got this thought partner that helps me get things done faster—and better.”

Designing with AI: A Coach’s New Creative Partner
One of Nova’s proudest recent projects is a five-part family learning series that she designed and facilitated herself. Each 90-minute session was framed around the experiential learning cycle, and rather than starting from scratch each time, Nova used ChatGPT to sketch out rough designs, brainstorm interactive structures, and refine readings and excerpts.
She gave the model her goals, facilitation structure, and tone. It came back with outlines and activity options. From there, Nova did what she does best—iterate, adapt, and bring human-centered life to the experience.
“I told it: here’s what I want, here’s the flow. Then I say, ‘I like this, I don’t like that.’ It’s like co-designing with a super fast assistant who doesn’t need sleep.”

From Facilitator to Camping Trip Coordinator (Yes, Really)
Nova also took on a wildly different challenge: coordinating her school’s annual 4th grade “survival” camping trip for over 100 kids. Students request their tentmates, and the task of sorting everyone into balanced, compliant groupings is traditionally a six-hour paper shuffle. Not this year.
“I’ve been using GPT to generate grouping iterations with all the constraints built in. It’s been… interesting. It broke the model. I mean, it literally crashed GPT at one point.”
What followed was a hilarious and instructive example of Nova’s evolving AI fluency. At one point, GPT claimed to have produced an export file. The file didn’t exist. Frustrated, Nova’s husband Charles (a fellow GPT enthusiast) suggested a brilliant move:
“Ask if it’s hallucinating.”
She did. GPT confessed. The revised link worked.
That moment crystallized something deeper: learning to use AI isn’t just about getting the right answer—it’s about learning how to learn with it. How to ask sharper questions. How to clarify structure. How to think differently.

The Learner’s Stance: Curious, Critical, and Capable
Nova’s relationship with AI mirrors the learner stance she hopes students and coaches alike can embody: open, discerning, iterative, and bold. She’s not waiting for a curriculum on AI fluency—she’s building it by doing.
Even when she gets stuck or the tool doesn’t behave as expected, she keeps showing up with curiosity and a sense of humor. That’s what makes her work with AI feel so alive. She treats the model like a new assistant: eager but imperfect, needing guidance, trust, and well-crafted parameters.
“Charles helped me realize—start a clean thread, give it clear parameters upfront, be precise about your ask. It works better when I do that. But I had to learn how to give it clarity by practicing.”

Looking Ahead
As Nova explores new possibilities—from coaching coaches to joining international learning teams—her fluency with AI becomes more than a productivity hack. It’s a core part of her evolving educator identity.
She’s not just asking what AI can do for her. She’s asking how AI can partner with her—how it can extend her reach, deepen her practice, and mirror the kind of reflective, generative thinking she wants for all learners.
“We don’t even know yet what kids are going to do with this. They’re AI natives. Honestly, we need to start asking them what they think their role is going to be.”
Nova Katz is learning out loud. And she’s not just preparing for the future—she’s shaping it.', 'idea', '2025-04-24 18:19:00.787614+00', '2025-04-24 18:19:00.787614+00', NULL, NULL),
	('18d52890-18d5-4392-8a0d-4dcf4e756c76', '5a8ff088-2eb6-45c7-84d7-d40f066b7a20', NULL, 'Who''s Who: David Olson
Role:
 Fifth Grade Math and Science Teacher, Manson Elementary (Manson, WA)
Background:
David Olson transitioned into teaching after a 15-year career as a mechanical engineer. His industry experience shapes a teaching philosophy focused on empowering students to take initiative in their learning and preparing them for complex future careers.
Three Quotes or Ideas to Think About:
"I''m trying to empower children to take initiative with their education."​


"Teaching is a second career for me... I really want to instill that love of learning so that they can be lifelong learners."​


"I really like to have number talks with the kids. It''s great to see how everyone''s brain works differently to solve a similar problem."​


Fun Fact:
When he’s not teaching, David loves being outdoors—especially fishing in all its forms: ice fishing, fly fishing, and spin fishing.', 'idea', '2025-04-25 22:21:44.649116+00', '2025-04-25 22:21:44.649116+00', NULL, NULL),
	('231e75fc-8397-465a-b7bc-21f8eb53b8b5', '45a4fa1a-ba74-4b76-b607-072b45ea750b', NULL, 'Tiana Brourne
Secondary Educator • AI PD Curator • Real-World Learning Advocate

“Teachers are pivotal in making sure that all their students get exactly what they need in their classroom.”

Tiana Brourne brings energy, intention, and practicality to everything she touches. A secondary educator and professional development leader, she’s currently partnering with her district to design ways teachers can meaningfully integrate AI—supporting both themselves and their students. In her words, the next 90 days are about ensuring that educators aren''t just using AI, but using it well.

Tiana believes deeply in every student’s right to learn and sees teaching as a calling to meet young people where they are. “I always try to perfect my practice,” she shares, driven by a vision of helping students succeed both in school and in the world beyond. That belief comes alive through lessons that bridge academic standards and essential life skills. On “Business Fridays,” for instance, her students take on weekly budgeting challenges using real grocery prices—a hands-on practice in adulting that resonates with her learners.

At heart, Tiana is both grounded and joyful. She lights up talking about binge-worthy TV (“I know it’s a bad hobby,” she laughs, “but I love it”) and can name reality shows and series like Equalizer and Pretty Little Liars with equal enthusiasm. These moments of downtime don’t distract from her mission—they fuel her creativity and remind her what makes students tick.

Three Ideas to Think About from Tiana''s Reflection:
AI as an Empowering Tool: She frames AI not as a threat, but as an opportunity for both educators and students to thrive.

Everyday Relevance: Tiana''s lessons connect academic learning with life readiness, underscoring the value of practical, lived experience in the classroom.

Dedication to Growth: Her iterative mindset—constantly refining her practice—is a model of professional learning and student-centered design.', 'idea', '2025-04-30 15:06:20.252984+00', '2025-04-30 15:06:20.252984+00', NULL, NULL),
	('b497f7d4-53ee-484c-a829-6c2d282c7ce7', 'f79ffe36-4f02-4ba8-a359-b9bd8963cbd1', NULL, 'You are experimenting in a moment when the rules aren’t clear yet. That kind of courage creates new pathways for others too. Your work with AI is already making a difference, with this partner today and with your students.', 'idea', '2025-04-28 16:29:30.619382+00', '2025-04-28 16:29:40.229279+00', 3, 3),
	('b0aaa8b4-77aa-414f-bd7e-54305a7d48c1', 'f79ffe36-4f02-4ba8-a359-b9bd8963cbd1', NULL, '"When I''m using a gpt I''ve created, it helps me remember what I''ve committed to, you know? In a lesson plan or when I''m grading projects, my gpt holds me accountable too." -- SHARE YOUR WISE WORDS! It''s a great insight and helps people understand the WHY of this tool.', 'idea', '2025-04-28 16:23:08.867948+00', '2025-04-28 16:23:29.32868+00', 3, 3),
	('0f019070-20c5-4882-b13d-ce2c52756b71', 'f79ffe36-4f02-4ba8-a359-b9bd8963cbd1', NULL, 'Remember the power of checking in with REAL EDUCATORS about how this stuff works for you: "Honestly, even just a 10-minute chat gave me more clarity than an hour of planning alone."', 'idea', '2025-04-28 16:27:32.57343+00', '2025-04-28 16:27:44.036674+00', 3, 3),
	('ff3295be-121c-4c2b-a115-65cfb72abd76', 'e6a3a52f-5383-4756-9c91-2b4068ee26e3', NULL, 'Yousuf Marvi — Building Bridges Between Equity, Rigor, and Human Connection
Yousuf Marvi is a middle school math teacher with a rich background spanning elementary through high school, curriculum coaching, and educational technology. With roots in both teaching and tech, Yousuf has spent the last five years immersing himself in AI—not as an end, but as a means to a more learner-centered future.

“What I’m really curious about is how do we use AI from a learner perspective—where the learner comes first, and the solution comes later.”

He brings this inquiry to life through district-level leadership, serving on an Academic Honesty Committee shaping AI policy for over 30,000 students and spearheading the district’s first student-facing AI implementation. His vision insists on rigor without sacrificing humanity: equitable access to high-quality instruction that doesn’t compromise the teacher-student relationship.

“Not just more worksheets in the name of differentiation... but using AI to deepen the teacher-student relationship rather than compromise it.”

Yousuf sees teaching as a spectrum—highly contextual, always grounded in the community’s aspirations and needs. Whether working in Title 1 schools or privileged districts, he holds steady to one charge:

“My job is to make the learning happen—to find ways for that transformational change, not just within a student, but within the family and the larger community as well.”', 'idea', '2025-04-30 15:10:23.766858+00', '2025-04-30 15:10:23.766858+00', NULL, NULL),
	('b2e8076c-5d2e-442d-9327-c38608d9fbf9', 'f79ffe36-4f02-4ba8-a359-b9bd8963cbd1', NULL, 'YESSS. You are treating AI like a tool, not a replacement for your judgment. Keep trusting your judgment here, that point clearly resonated with your partner too. You''re helping model what real, thoughtful adoption looks like.', 'idea', '2025-04-28 16:31:58.218698+00', '2025-04-28 16:32:08.964437+00', 3, 3),
	('1b80e6be-bbd4-4a70-a180-e8a228b1ff41', 'f79ffe36-4f02-4ba8-a359-b9bd8963cbd1', NULL, '"Sometimes just saying it out loud helps me realize what I actually believe about using ChatGPT." - KEEP GOING. Keep sharing and having important conversations (here and with your teacher team).', 'idea', '2025-04-28 16:36:10.422897+00', '2025-04-28 16:36:10.422897+00', NULL, NULL);


